:\n\n### Step 1: Add interaction features and transformations\nCombine relevant features using arithmetic operations and apply transformations to better capture non-linear patterns in the data.\n\n```python\n# debt_to_income_ratio\ndf[\'debt_to_income_ratio\'] = df[\'Debt\'] / (df[\'Income\'] + 1e-5) # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\n# Feature description: Ratio of debt to income. This indicates the proportion of a person\'s income going towards debt repayment.\n# Usefulness: High debt-to-income ratios often signify financial stress, which could influence credit card approval.\n\n# age_squared\ndf[\'age_squared\'] = df[\'Age\'] ** 2\n# Feature description: Square of the age.\n# Usefulness: Captures non-linear relationships between age and credit card approval. E.g., people in their prime earning age (30s-40s) may have higher approval rates compared to younger/older groups.\n\n# interaction_years_employed_income\ndf[\'interaction_years_employed_income\'] = df[\'YearsEmployed\'] * df[\'Income\']\n# Feature description: Interaction term between years employed and income.\n# Usefulness: Longer employment and higher income may correlate with financial stability and thus influence credit card approval.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: One-hot encode categorical columns\nTransform categorical variables into binary indicator variables to allow models to utilize these features properly.\n\n```python\n# One-hot encode \'Industry\'\ndf = pd.concat([df, pd.get_dummies(df[\'Industry\'], prefix=\'Industry\')], axis=1)\n# Feature description: One-hot encoding of job industry.\n# Usefulness: Different industries might have varying levels of financial stability or creditworthiness. E.g., highly stable industries such as "Education" might correlate with credit approval.\n\n# One-hot encode \'Ethnicity\'\ndf = pd.concat([df, pd.get_dummies(df[\'Ethnicity\'], prefix=\'Ethnicity\')], axis=1)\n# Feature description: One-hot encoding of ethnicity.\n# Usefulness: Although ethical considerations must be taken into account, ethnicity could serve as a proxy for regional or socio-economic factors influencing credit worthiness.\n\n# One-hot encode \'Citizen\'\ndf = pd.concat([df, pd.get_dummies(df[\'Citizen\'], prefix=\'Citizen\')], axis=1)\n# Feature description: One-hot encoding of citizenship status.\n# Usefulness: Citizenship by birth or other methods may affect credit card approval likelihood due to legal and residency requirements.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Target encoding and binning discrete numerical features\nUse target encoding and binning strategies for numerical features that might exhibit grouped correlations with the target variable.\n\n```python\n# target_encoding_prior_default\nprior_default_mean_target = df.groupby(\'PriorDefault\')[\'Answer\'].mean()\ndf[\'prior_default_encoded\'] = df[\'PriorDefault\'].map(prior_default_mean_target)\n# Feature description: Mean target encoding of prior default.\n# Usefulness: Encodes statistical likelihood of approval based on whether a person has a prior default, capturing immediate relationships with the target variable.\n\n# employment_bin\ndf[\'employment_bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'YearsEmployed\'], bins=[0, 1, 3, 10, float(\'inf\')], labels=[\'<1yr\', \'1-3yrs\', \'3-10yrs\', \'>10yrs\'])\n# Feature description: Binned years of employment.\n# Usefulness: Binning might reveal distinct trends, such as distinguishing between new employees (<1yr) versus experienced (>10yrs) professionals, which impacts credit card approval.\n\n# credit_score_bin\ndf[\'credit_score_bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'CreditScore\'], bins=[-1, 0, 10, 30, float(\'inf\')], labels=[\'NoScore\', \'Low\', \'Medium\', \'High\'])\n# Feature description: Binned credit score.\n# Usefulness: Binning credit scores simplifies their use while still preserving meaningful group differences (e.g., no credit vs. good credit vs. bad credit).\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping unnecessary columns\nBased on redundancy or irrelevance, the following columns can be dropped:\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'Industry\', \'Ethnicity\', and \'Citizen\' were dropped\ndf.drop(columns=[\'Industry\', \'Ethnicity\', \'Citizen\'], inplace=True)\n# These columns have been one-hot encoded, making the original categorical columns redundant.\n\n# Explanation why the column \'ZipCode\' is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=[\'ZipCode\'], inplace=True)\n# ZipCode is unlikely to directly add value given that it is sparse, high-dimensional, and less interpretable for this context. Any potential signal will be found in other features (like socioeconomic or industry-related features).\n```end']
